<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=germ-theory-of-disease">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germ theory of disease</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Germ theory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The germ theory of disease is the scientific theory that microorganisms, known as pathogens or germs, can cause disease; Athanasius Kircher was the first to attribute infectious disease to a microscopic pathogen, in his Scrutinium Physico-Medicum, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1658. Basic forms of the theory had been proposed by Girolamo Fracastoro in 1546. It took hold only towards the end of the 19th century, replacing the miasma theory that diseases came from bad air, after the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-pasteur">Louis Pasteur</a> from the late 1850s and of Robert Koch in the 1880s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germ-theory-of-disease.jpg" alt="Germ theory of disease" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swan-necked_flask_used_by_Pasteur._Wellcome_M0012521.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1658</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Rome</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Athanasius Kircher</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Girolamo Fracastoro proposed a basic form in 1546; the theory prevailed only after the work of Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch in the late 19th century.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">Microscope<span>c. 1620</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/antiseptic-surgery">Antiseptic surgery<span>1850s</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/pasteurisation">Pasteurisation<span>1864</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/penicillin">Penicillin<span>1928</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=germ-theory-of-disease">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
